<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors in Our World</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's an Orange Cat.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Identify a few familiar words from a story by connecting them to prior knowledge. Concept: Use a word and a gesture or drawing to describe a familiar object.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use simple drawings to communicate about objects (e.g., drawing a blue bird). Can use gestures or point to objects to clarify their responses when describing colors. Can describe objects and concepts from a story by connecting prior knowledge with supportive strategies, such as using words and gestures (e.g., saying "bird" while flapping arms).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Color Guessing Game." The teacher holds up flashcards of previously learned objects (e.g., a blue bird, a red flower, an orange cat, a green tree). Students chorally say the color and object, "It's a blue bird!" or "It's an orange cat!" This activates prior knowledge of colors and objects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target language: "What color is it? It is a [color] [object]." The teacher demonstrates clear pronunciation, focusing on /l/ in "blue" and "leaf." The teacher also models using gestures, e.g., flapping arms for "bird," making a 'meow' sound for "cat," and pointing to colors.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple Concept Checking Questions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a red flower flashcard) "Is this a blue flower? Yes or no?" (Expected: No!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to an orange cat flashcard) "What color is it? Is it green or orange?" (Expected: Orange!)</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher uses gestures and pointing to aid understanding during CCQs.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors in Our World."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, illustrated storybook or a sequence of large picture cards titled "Colors in Our World." The story features simple sentences using target grammar (Color + noun, Present simple) and vocabulary (e.g., "See the blue bird? It flies. Look! A red flower. A green tree. It is an orange cat. This is my house, it is red and blue!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Recognizing colors and objects: Identify familiar objects by color (e.g., "blue bird" or "red flower."). Describing with colors: Use simple color phrases in sentences (e.g., "That is a red bird.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the story about? Is it about numbers or colors?" (Expected: Colors!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The cat is blue. True or False?" (Teacher waits for thumbs up/down.) "The flower is red. True or False?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher names a color and an object (e.g., "blue bird"). Students point to the corresponding picture in the story.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using Color + noun (e.g., "blue bird"), WH-questions ("What color is it?"), and Present simple ("It is an orange cat."). Vocabulary: bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky; blue, purple, orange, green, red.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice (Matching and Gesturing):</b> The teacher says a phrase like "It is a blue bird." Students point to the blue bird flashcard/picture AND perform a gesture (e.g., flapping arms for 'bird'). The teacher says "It is a red flower." Students point AND make a gesture of a flower blooming. Students also practice the initial sounds /b/ in "bird", /k/ in "cat."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled (Drawing and Naming):</b> The teacher draws a simple, recognizable object (e.g., a tree, a house) on the board. Students identify the object: "It's a tree!" The teacher asks, "What color is it?" Students respond with a color (e.g., "Green!"). The teacher then adds the color to the drawing. Then, students get a small whiteboard or paper. The teacher names an object, and students draw it simply (e.g., a bird). Then, the teacher names a color (e.g., "blue"). Students color their drawing. Finally, students show their drawing and say, "It's a blue bird!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair interaction (Describing with Gestures/Drawings):</b> In pairs, students receive a set of flashcards with colored objects (e.g., orange cat, blue bird, green leaf, red flower). Student A picks a card secretly and describes the object to Student B using only a simple word (e.g., "cat," "bird") and a gesture (e.g., meowing, flapping arms) or a very quick, simple drawing on a mini-whiteboard. Student B guesses the complete phrase: "It's an orange cat!" or "It's a blue bird!" Students take turns. This activity encourages mediation through non-verbal and simplified verbal communication.</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(15 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors in Our World."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will demonstrate and explain parts of their "Color Sorting Game" project in small groups. They will mediate information about colors and objects using simple language and supportive gestures/actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students) and are given a large "Color Sorting Chart" (a poster with large squares of blue, red, green, orange) and a pile of small, pre-cut colored object pictures (e.g., several blue birds, red flowers, green leaves, orange cats, purple grapes).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">First, students collaboratively sort the colored object pictures onto the correct color squares on their chart. The teacher circulates, observing and providing support.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, within their groups, each student takes a turn to pick two sorted objects from their chart and explain/show where they belong. They must use a word and a gesture/pointing action to mediate the information. For example, a student picks a blue bird and a red flower: they hold up the blue bird, say "Blue bird!", flap their arms, and point to the blue section of the chart. Then they pick the red flower, say "Red flower!", make a flower gesture, and point to the red section.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This encourages students to mediate information about the objects and their colors, demonstrating how the game works using both verbal and non-verbal cues.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Color Sorting Game" demonstration.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will individually select two colored object cut-outs from their sorted chart. They will hold up one object, say its color and name (e.g., "blue bird"), make a relevant gesture (e.g., flapping arms for "bird"), and place it on the correct color section of the chart. They repeat for the second object. This demonstrates their ability to mediate and explain using words and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of colors and object names (e.g., "blue," "red," "cat," "bird," /l/ in "blue").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., "blue bird," "red flower," "orange cat").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation/demonstration using appropriate gestures or pointing to mediate the connection between object, color, and sorting location.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to correctly sort and identify at least two colored objects.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw one of their favorite colored objects from the game and color it. Then they complete these simple sentences:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can show a [color] [object]." (e.g., "I can show a blue bird.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can use my hands to show a [object]." (e.g., "I can use my hands to show a cat.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(8 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to show with your hands today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color words did you like to say?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What activities worked best for helping students mediate information using words and gestures/drawings? Were the instructions for the "Color Sorting Game" clear for 1st graders? Did students demonstrate the learning outcomes effectively? What adjustments might be needed for similar mediation tasks in future lessons (e.g., more examples of gestures, clearer visual aids)?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> (This is the final lesson in the sequence. The connection would lead to a new theme/scenario.) The next lesson will introduce new vocabulary and a new scenario, building on the students' ability to describe objects and use supportive strategies for communication.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>